# The Myth of the Two Rivers

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each village had a magical spring that produced five cups of water daily. The valley between them was cursed—any water poured into it would triple in volume by the time it reached the other side.

The villages suffered from drought. One day, a brave villager from the East poured all five cups into the valley. When fifteen cups reached the West village, they rejoiced and shared their bounty, sending back seven cups. These became twenty-one cups for the East, who then sent back ten.

As the seasons turned, both villages learned the sacred rhythm of giving and returning. They discovered that generosity multiplied not just water, but trust. Each village knew that sending their precious resource across the valley would be honored with fair return.

The curse became a blessing. The valley floor grew green with flowing streams. The villages prospered, learning that abundance flows to those who trust enough to give, and who honor that trust with fair return.

**The moral: What we send forth multiplied returns to us, but only when trust is honored with reciprocity.**
